        one_liner: "In Epigenetics, Wallach's entry for dysprosium -- a rare-earth metal -- covers only its natural distribution (parts-per-million across igneous rocks, shale, sandstone and limestone, with animal concentrations highest in bone) and its discovery history; he assigns it no specific human deficiency disease or nutritional function here. This reflects how little is individually established about the rare-earth trace minerals, which Wallach groups broadly as life-supporting elements while saying little element-by-element. Dysprosium was named in 1886 by the French chemist Paul Emile Lecoq de Boisbaudran from the Greek 'dysprositos', meaning 'hard to get', and is never found free in nature.",
