        facet: "protocol",
        question: "Why does Wallach say to salt your food?",
        answer_short: "Salt (NaCl) is the raw material for stomach acid; salt-restricted diets breed achlorhydria and failed absorption -- so his treatment starts with salting your food to taste.",
        answer: "Wallach identifies salt (sodium chloride) as the raw material the stomach needs to manufacture its acid, so people on salt-restricted diets are more prone to achlorhydria (loss of stomach acid). Achlorhydria brings 'burp, belch, and bloat,' lets intestinal bacteria and yeast ferment high-carbohydrate foods in the stomach, and leads to B12, calcium, and protein deficiencies plus new adult-onset food allergies from absorbing partly digested proteins -- so his treatment starts with putting salt back on your food.",
        verbatim: "Sodium chloride is the\nraw material required to manufacture stomach acid, so it follows that people\non a salt restricted diet are more likely to\ndevelop achlorhydria.\nSymptoms include \u201Cburp, belch, and bloat\u201D after eating. The reduction or\nabsence of acid in the stomach allows intestinal bacteria and yeast (Candida\nalbicans) to enter the stomach and ferment high carbohydrate foods (i.e.\njuice, fruit, breads, etc.). Failure to deal with achlorhydria will result in\nB12, calcium, and protein deficiencies and the onset of new food allergies\nacquired later in life as a result of absorption of partially digested\npolypeptides (relatively large protein fragments) which trigger an allergic\nresponse.\nTreatment of achlorhydria includes salting your food to taste and\nsupplementation of betaine HC1 at the rate of 75-250 mg 15-20 minutes\nbefore each meal.",
        page: null,
        book_id: "dddl-3e-2011",
        topics: [
          "salt",
          "stomach-acid",
          "hydrochloric-acid",
          "achlorhydria",
          "salt-restricted-diet",
          "betaine-hcl"
        ],
