        facet: "warning",
        question: "What can these minerals NOT do on their own?",
        answer_short: "Wallach's own honest limit on Mineral Toddy: every mineral in it is present only in tiny amounts. To hit even mainstream RDAs for calcium, magnesium, manganese, zinc, and potassium, you need a separate major-mineral source alongside it.",
        answer: `Wallach's own honest limit on Mineral Toddy, stated a few sentences after listing what it fixed. The colloidal liquid, he says, carries every mineral only in trace amounts. That is enough for the trace minerals the body needs in tiny amounts, but to even come close to the mainstream Recommended Daily Allowances it has to be paired with a separate source of what he calls the "major minerals and electrolytes": calcium, magnesium, manganese, zinc, and potassium. In the same passage he put the minerals in calcium-enriched orange juice, which both masked their harsh astringent taste and provided a significant amount of additional calcium \u2014 one of those lacking majors. On his own account the colloidal minerals were never meant as a stand-alone mineral supplement \u2014 a piece of the picture, not the whole picture.`,
