        facet: "mechanism",
        question: "How does fattening beef cattle explain human obesity in Wallach's view?",
        answer_short: "Wallach's 'Feedlot Syndrome' analogy: ranchers fatten beef cattle on a steady stream of carbohydrate feed paired with optimal nutrients that drive the animal to eat with endless, fervent hunger, growing a 40-lb calf to 900-1,100 lb in a year. He argues humans are fattened by the same carb-heavy diet \u2014 but, unlike the optimally-supplemented cattle, humans eat it while mineral-deficient, which is what leaves them with a ravenous appetite.",
        answer: "Wallach's 'Feedlot Syndrome' analogy: ranchers fatten beef cattle on a steady stream of carbohydrate feed paired with optimal nutrients that drive the animal to eat with endless, fervent hunger, growing a 40-lb calf to 900-1,100 lb in a year. He argues humans are fattened by the same carb-heavy diet \u2014 but, unlike the optimally-supplemented cattle, humans eat it while mineral-deficient, which is what leaves them with a ravenous appetite.",
        verbatim: "Raising prime beef successfully, requires a steady stream of car- \nbohydrate, protein and fat calories in the proper ratios specifically \ndesigned for fattening. \nThe intense weight gain, high carbohydrate diet must be accom- \npanied by optimal mixes of nutrients designed to encourage weight \ngain and drive the animal to eat with an endless and fervent hunger. \nFeed, based on corn or other carbohydrates, is made available in \na steady stream. Beef cattle will grow from a forty pound calf to 900 \n- 1,100 pounds in about a year under optimal feedlot conditions.",
