        question: "What is a colloidal mineral?",
        answer_short: "A mineral existing as ultra-fine particles (1\u2013100 nanometers \u2014 'nano-technology') suspended in liquid, too small to dissolve into single atoms the way salt does.",
        answer: "What is a colloidal mineral - and what does 'nano-technology' have to do with it? Wallach describes a colloid as a substance existing as ultra-fine particles suspended in a medium of different matter - so small (about one billion would fit into 0.01 cubic inch) that they cannot be seen with the naked eye. In the colloidal state, he says, the particles remain grouped together instead of separating into individual atoms the way salt does in a true solution. He notes colloids remain heterogeneous, multiphasic and insoluble as long as a larger number of the particles, if not all, stay within the colloidal size range of 1nm to 100nm - a range, he points out, known as 'nano-technology.'",
        verbatim: "The interesting\nthing about colloids is that they remain heterogeneous, multiphasic and insoluble at different concentrations as long as a\nlarger number if not all of the particles are within a range of\nsizes of colloids (1nm to 100nm) known as \u201Cnano-technology.\u201D",
