        subject: "potassium",
        also_about: [],
        facet: "basics",
        question: "What is potassium, chemically?",
        answer_short: "A silver-white alkali metal so soft you can cut it with a knife - and is never found pure in nature; Davy first isolated it by electrolysis in 1807.",
        answer: "What potassium is, chemically. It is a silver-white metal so soft it can easily be cut with a knife, and a member of the alkali-metal family alongside lithium and sodium \u2014 like them, it is never found as a pure metal in nature. It was first isolated by Sir Humphrey Davy in 1807 using electrolysis (passing an electric current to split it out of its compounds).",
        verbatim: "Potassium is a silver-white metal with a characteristic\nputty or wax-like nature that is so soft that it can easily be cut\nwith a knife. It is a member of the family of alkali metals along\nwith the elements lithium and sodium, and like them is never\nfound as a pure metal in nature.",
